Sometimes there are inconsistencies that exist as such only because not
enough is known.  Nethack seems to be full of them -- the world isn't
inconsistent, you just don't know enough (yet) to know why.

Oracle says: Not all fires are equal.
Oracle says: Sometimes a fire is just a fire... and sometimes not.

etc.

It is not necessary that a *new* player be able to predict what will
happen, but an experienced one should be able to.  If you consider that
most of the truly scary fire options don't come up until the (typical)
player has a moderate amount of experience, it shouldn't be a big deal.

Consider, most fire in the early game is probably fire traps.  Fireball
doesn't show up much, wands of fire do (pesky gnomes) but you could
explain that with "the fire overcomes your resistance!" to indicate
something unobvious is going on, red dragons only if you get lucky with
a bones file or something.

The rule could be as simple as "if it exists IRL, fire resistance works
on it; otherwise, it doesn't".  It would work for fire traps, burning
oil, and lava.  Elementals, fire vortexes (maybe... they happen in
forest fires but aren't creatures there), wands of fire and red dragons
don't exist IRL, so fire resistance doesn't work there.  Plane of fire
is a tricky one, since you could rule that it's a plane of natural fire,
or that it's a magical plane of magical fire, etc.  I'd be inclined to
make it natural -- someone with fire resistance would be safe from the
plane itself, but still want to avoid the elementals.

Gehennom, OTOH, could be soulfire -- no protection whatsoever (the only
times I've been there, I've had fire resistance, so I don't know if
there's actually ambient damage going on).


